---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import DisciplineSwitch from "../../components/shared/DisciplineSwitch.astro";
import ProductionsSubNav from "../../components/productions/ProductionsSubNav.astro";
import { projects } from "../../data/three-d";
import { srcset, dims } from "../../lib/responsive";

const productionsList = projects.filter((p) => p.category === "productions");
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Graphic Design & 3D"
  description="3D and graphic design work by Alexandre Haineaux — character modelling, rigging, and texturing for animated television, alongside personal studies."
>
  <h1 class="sr-only">Productions</h1>

  <!-- Paper carried down from the top of the viewport, behind the fixed nav
       pill and the sticky sub-nav.

       THIS IS LOAD-BEARING, not decoration. Both pills are deliberately
       translucent so they can sit over content, and this is the first page
       whose artwork bleeds under them: every band scrolls up through that
       strip. Without this fade, a dark or busy crop passing underneath leaves
       the pill's hairline ring as the only thing separating the navigation
       from the picture, and the labels stop being readable — silently, only
       while scrolling, so it does not show up in a screenshot of the page at
       rest. See CLAUDE.md, "Never put an image under the nav pill".

       The stop positions are measured, not picked: the sub-nav rests with its
       bottom edge at ~141px (5.5rem spacer + its own height), so the fade
       holds full paper to 150px and only then starts to clear. z-index 39
       puts it under the sub-nav (40) and the nav (50) and over the bands.
       Re-measure if the spacer or either pill's padding changes.

       TWO layers, and they have to stay SIBLINGS. An element carrying a mask
       or a backdrop-filter is a Backdrop Root, and a backdrop-filter on a
       DESCENDANT of one samples only that ancestor's own painting rather
       than the page behind it. Nest these, or wrap them in a container that
       picks up a mask, an opacity or a filter of its own, and the inner
       layer blurs flat colour and silently does nothing. -->
  <div class="masthead-lens" aria-hidden="true"></div>
  <div class="masthead-fade" aria-hidden="true"></div>

  <div class="subnav-spacer" aria-hidden="true"></div>
  <ProductionsSubNav active="productions" />

  <!-- Chapters: one full-bleed band per project, the title set over the crop. -->
  <section class="chapters" aria-label="Projects">
    {productionsList.map((project, i) => {
      const d = dims(project.cover);
      return (
        <a href={`/productions/${project.slug}`} class="chapter">
          <img
            src={project.cover}
            srcset={srcset(project.cover)}
            sizes="100vw"
            alt={`${project.title} — Graphic Design & 3D project`}
            width={d?.w ?? 1600}
            height={d?.h ?? 1000}
            loading={i === 0 ? "eager" : "lazy"}
            fetchpriority={i === 0 ? "high" : undefined}
            decoding="async"
            class="chapter__img"
          />

          <div class="chapter__scrim" aria-hidden="true"></div>

          <div class="chapter__body editorial">
            <p class="chapter__kicker">{`${String(i + 1).padStart(2, "0")} — Chapter`}</p>
            <h2 class="chapter__title">{project.title}</h2>
            <p class="chapter__tags">{project.tags.slice(0, 2).join(" · ")}</p>
            <span class="chapter__cta">View project →</span>
          </div>
        </a>
      );
    })}
  </section>

  <DisciplineSwitch label="Also explore" href="/photography" name="Photography" />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .subnav-spacer { height: 5.5rem; }

  /* ── The masthead glass ───────────────────────────────────────
     WHY this strip exists is in the comment above the markup. This is what
     it is MADE of: two stacked layers, so the glass has an optical thickness
     that reaches further down the page than its paper does.

       .masthead-lens (z 38)  blur only, no tint.  full to 8rem → 11.5rem
       .masthead-fade (z 39)  the frost: blur + saturation + paper.
                                                   full to 9.375rem → 10.375rem

     Painted in that order, so the frost's own blur samples the lens's output
     and the two compound (~21px at the top) instead of reading as two sheets.

     A masked backdrop-filter is NOT a blur that stops — it is the blurred
     backdrop cross-faded with the sharp one at the mask's alpha. That is what
     turns two flat layers into one graduated material, and it is the whole
     trick here: the paper still ends crisply at 10.375rem while focus keeps
     coming back for another ~18px underneath it, so a band resolves out of
     the glass rather than switching.

     THE MASKS ARE NOT OPTIONAL. `backdrop-filter` cannot be graduated — it is
     either on for the whole element or off — so fading the tint with a
     gradient background alone would leave the BLUR stopping dead on a
     horizontal line across the viewport. Masking attenuates tint and blur
     together, which is the only way to get one soft edge.

     Lengths, not percentages, so the geometry survives a change of height.
     Do not stretch the frost's 16px ramp back out: the long ramp it replaced
     washed the top of the first band grey for no gain, and the lens is what
     does that job now — without adding paper, which is why it can be long.
     Do not take the frost's ramp below ~12px either.

     SATURATION IS THE POINT, not decoration. Frosted glass desaturates what
     is behind it, and paper over a 20px blur was turning a chapter cover into
     grey mush — the opposite of what the comment above asks this strip for
     ("visible underneath as drifting colour"). saturate() runs on the
     backdrop BEFORE the paper composites over it, so the colour survives the
     trip. Push it past ~180% and the crops go radioactive under the pills.

     CONTRAST. The two pills occupy 0–141px and carry their own paper on top
     of this (0.72 nav, 0.80 sub-nav), so what actually backs a nav label is
     the composite of the two. The density curve below is set for that: it
     opens DENSER than the flat 0.82 it replaced and thins only below where
     the pills end, so every label sits on at least as much paper as before.
     Re-measure the composited pixels if either pill's opacity or this curve
     moves — never from a screenshot, which paints this strip over the whole
     capture and reports failures that are not there. */
  .masthead-lens,
  .masthead-fade {
    position: fixed;
    inset-inline: 0;
    top: 0;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  /* The reach. No background at all — this layer only softens, which is what
     lets it run past the paper without greying the artwork it covers. */
  .masthead-lens {
    z-index: 38;
    height: 11.5rem;
    /* Prefixed FIRST, standard last — see .masthead-fade below for why. */
    -webkit-backdrop-filter: blur(8px);
    backdrop-filter: blur(8px);
    -webkit-mask-image: linear-gradient(to bottom, #000 8rem, transparent 11.5rem);
    mask-image: linear-gradient(to bottom, #000 8rem, transparent 11.5rem);
  }

  .masthead-fade {
    z-index: 39;
    height: 10.375rem;
    /* Two backgrounds: sheen over density.

       The sheen is the one liquid cue in here — glass catches light unevenly
       across its width. It is pure white, so it can only ever ADD paper and
       can never cost a label its contrast, and at 6–10% it is invisible over
       an empty page. It shows only when something dark passes under it,
       which on this page means while a chapter band is scrolling through.

       The density curve runs 0.90 → 0.79 down the strip: heaviest where the
       nav pill needs backing, lightest at 9.375rem, where nothing sits on it
       and the artwork should come closest to reading through. */
    background:
      linear-gradient(105deg,
        rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.10) 0%,
        rgba(255, 255, 255, 0)    46%,
        rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.06) 78%,
        rgba(255, 255, 255, 0)    100%),
      linear-gradient(to bottom,
        rgba(252, 252, 250, 0.90) 0,
        rgba(250, 250, 248, 0.84) 4.75rem,
        rgba(247, 246, 243, 0.79) 9.375rem);
    /* Prefixed FIRST, standard last. Reversed, the minifier collapses the
       pair to `-webkit-backdrop-filter` alone — and Firefox does not support
       that alias (`CSS.supports('-webkit-backdrop-filter', …)` is false
       there), so the panel would ship to Firefox as flat paper with the
       artwork sharp behind it rather than frosted. */
    -webkit-backdrop-filter: blur(20px) saturate(160%);
    backdrop-filter: blur(20px) saturate(160%);
    -webkit-mask-image: linear-gradient(to bottom, #000 9.375rem, transparent 10.375rem);
    mask-image: linear-gradient(to bottom, #000 9.375rem, transparent 10.375rem);
  }

  /* Reduced transparency asks for surfaces, not layers: drop the lens and
     serve the frost as plain paper. The mask stays, so the strip still ends
     on a soft edge instead of a rule drawn across the page. */
  @media (prefers-reduced-transparency: reduce) {
    .masthead-lens { display: none; }

    .masthead-fade {
      background: var(--color-paper);
      -webkit-backdrop-filter: none;
      backdrop-filter: none;
    }
  }

  /* ── Chapters ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Deliberately NOT `.editorial` — the bands run the full width of the
     viewport, and only the text inside each one is held to the editorial
     measure. */
  .chapters { padding-block: 3rem 0; }

  .chapter {
    position: relative;
    display: block;
    /* 21vw lands the band at ~300px on a 1440 viewport, and the clamp holds
       it readable either side: 240px on a phone, capped at 352px so a wide
       desktop does not turn five projects into five screens. */
    height: clamp(15rem, 21vw, 22rem);
    overflow: hidden;
    text-decoration: none;
    /* A hairline of paper between bands, so two dark crops meeting do not
       read as one continuous image. */
    box-shadow: inset 0 -1px 0 rgba(250, 250, 248, 0.2);
  }

  /* The band is the whole link, so the global focus ring — which draws
     OUTSIDE the box — would be clipped at the viewport edges. Drawn inward
     instead, with a paper gap so it survives a busy crop. */
  .chapter:focus-visible {
    box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 3px var(--color-paper),
                inset 0 0 0 6px var(--color-accent-ink);
  }

  .chapter__img {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
    /* Four of the five covers are square masters; centring the crop on the
       upper-middle keeps a character's head in frame at this aspect. */
    object-position: center 42%;
    transition: transform 0.8s cubic-bezier(0.25, 0.46, 0.45, 0.94);
  }

  .chapter:hover .chapter__img { transform: scale(1.03); }

  /* Phone: the text stacks against the bottom edge, so the scrim runs upward
     and has to stay strong through the whole block — the kicker sits ~90px up
     from the base of a 240px band, and a gradient already thinning by there
     measured 2.0:1. These stops hold it above the AA floor for the full
     stack. */
  .chapter__scrim {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    z-index: 1;
    background: linear-gradient(
      to top,
      rgba(10, 9, 8, 0.92) 0%,
      rgba(10, 9, 8, 0.8)  42%,
      rgba(10, 9, 8, 0.36) 76%,
      rgba(10, 9, 8, 0.06) 100%
    );
  }

  .chapter__body {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    z-index: 2;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: flex-start;
    justify-content: flex-end;
    padding-block-end: 1.5rem;
  }

  /* Past the phone the scrim turns to run left-to-right and the text centres
     in the band — there is room beside the artwork rather than only under it,
     so the crop stays visible instead of being darkened along its full width.

     The stops are set by measurement, not taste. At 0.52/34% the white titles
     measured 2.7:1 over the pale crops (Crown, Rambochador) — under the 3:1
     WCAG AA floor for large text, and the pale ones are exactly the covers
     this layout cannot avoid. 0.62 held to 40% clears every band with margin
     while leaving the right half of each crop untouched. Re-measure if the
     scrim, the title size or the covers change. */
  @media (min-width: 640px) {
    .chapter__scrim {
      background: linear-gradient(
        to right,
        rgba(10, 9, 8, 0.86) 0%,
        rgba(10, 9, 8, 0.62) 40%,
        rgba(10, 9, 8, 0)    74%
      );
    }

    .chapter__body {
      justify-content: center;
      padding-block-end: 0;
    }
  }

  .chapter__kicker {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 0.6875rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: var(--letter-spacing-caps);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    /* 0.80, not the 0.66 the mockup carried: AZUREUS is the one title that
       wraps to two lines on a phone, which lifts the kicker into the thin end
       of the scrim and over the blue glow behind it. At 0.72 it measured
       4.34:1, just under the 4.5 floor for 11px text. */
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8);
  }

  .chapter__title {
    /* Held inside the dark end of the scrim — see the stop measurements
       above. Wider and the longest titles run into the bright half. */
    max-width: 32rem;
    margin: 0.5rem 0 0;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: clamp(1.625rem, 3.2vw, 2.875rem);
    line-height: 1.05;
    letter-spacing: -0.015em;
    color: #FFFFFF;
    text-wrap: balance;
    text-shadow: 0 1px 12px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.35);
  }

  .chapter__tags {
    margin: 0.625rem 0 0;
    font-size: 0.6875rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: var(--letter-spacing-caps);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.82);
  }

  /* Phone bands carry no call to action: the whole band is the link, and the
     four lines it would make do not fit above the fold of a 240px band
     without pushing the kicker up into the thin end of the scrim. */
  .chapter__cta {
    display: none;
    padding-bottom: 0.25rem;
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: #FFFFFF;
    border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.42);
    transition: border-color 0.25s;
  }

  .chapter:hover .chapter__cta { border-color: #FFFFFF; }

  @media (min-width: 640px) {
    .chapter__title { margin-top: 0.75rem; }
    .chapter__tags  { margin-top: 1rem; }
    .chapter__cta   { display: block; margin-top: 1.375rem; }
  }
</style>
